# Merge Sort Implementation in C with Detailed Comments

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

// Function to merge two sorted subarrays into a single sorted array
// arr[l..m] and arr[m+1..r] are sorted subarrays
void merge(int arr[], int l, int m, int r) {
    int n1 = m - l + 1;  // Size of left subarray
    int n2 = r - m;      // Size of right subarray

    // Create temporary arrays for left and right subarrays
    int* left = (int*)malloc(n1 * sizeof(int));
    int* right = (int*)malloc(n2 * sizeof(int));

    // Copy data to temporary arrays
    for (int i = 0; i < n1; i++) {
        left[i] = arr[l + i];
    }
    for (int j = 0; j < n2; j++) {
        right[j] = arr[m + 1 + j];
    }

    // Merge the temporary arrays back into arr[l..r]
    int i = 0;  // Initial index of left subarray
    int j = 0;  // Initial index of right subarray
    int k = l;  // Initial index of merged subarray

    // Compare elements from both arrays and place smaller one in arr
    while (i < n1 && j < n2) {
        if (left[i] <= right[j]) {
            arr[k] = left[i];
            i++;
        } else {
            arr[k] = right[j];
            j++;
        }
        k++;
    }

    // Copy remaining elements from left subarray, if any
    while (i < n1) {
        arr[k] = left[i];
        i++;
        k++;
    }

    // Copy remaining elements from right subarray, if any
    while (j < n2) {
        arr[k] = right[j];
        j++;
        k++;
    }

    // Free allocated memory to prevent memory leaks
    free(left);
    free(right);
}

// Merge Sort function: recursive implementation
void mergesort(int arr[], int l, int r) {
    // Base case: if array has 1 or 0 elements, it's already sorted
    if (l < r) {
        // Find the middle point to divide array into two halves
        int m = l + (r - l) / 2;

        // Recursively sort first and second halves
        mergesort(arr, l, m);
        mergesort(arr, m + 1, r);

        // Merge the sorted halves
        merge(arr, l, m, r);
    }
}

// Function to print array elements
void printArray(int arr[], int size) {
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);
    }
    printf("\n");
}

// Function to generate random array for testing
void generateRandomArray(int arr[], int size) {
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        arr[i] = rand() % 100;  // Random number between 0 and 99
    }
}

// Main function with test cases
int main() {
    // Test Case 1: Simple unsorted array
    int arr1[] = {64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90};
    int n1 = sizeof(arr1) / sizeof(arr1[0]);
    printf("Test Case 1 - Original array: ");
    printArray(arr1, n1);
    mergesort(arr1, 0, n1 - 1);
    printf("Test Case 1 - Sorted array:   ");
    printArray(arr1, n1);

    // Test Case 2: Array with duplicates
    int arr2[] = {5, 2, 8, 2, 9, 1, 5, 5};
    int n2 = sizeof(arr2) / sizeof(arr2[0]);
    printf("\nTest Case 2 - Original array: ");
    printArray(arr2, n2);
    mergesort(arr2, 0, n2 - 1);
    printf("Test Case 2 - Sorted array:   ");
    printArray(arr2, n2);

    // Test Case 3: Already sorted array
    int arr3[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7};
    int n3 = sizeof(arr3) / sizeof(arr3[0]);
    printf("\nTest Case 3 - Original array: ");
    printArray(arr3, n3);
    mergesort(arr3, 0, n3 - 1);
    printf("Test Case 3 - Sorted array:   ");
    printArray(arr3, n3);

    // Test Case 4: Reverse sorted array
    int arr4[] = {7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1};
    int n4 = sizeof(arr4) / sizeof(arr4[0]);
    printf("\nTest Case 4 - Original array: ");
    printArray(arr4, n4);
    mergesort(arr4, 0, n4 - 1);
    printf("Test Case 4 - Sorted array:   ");
    printArray(arr4, n4);

    // Test Case 5: Random array
    int arr5[10];
    generateRandomArray(arr5, 10);
    printf("\nTest Case 5 - Random array:   ");
    printArray(arr5, 10);
    mergesort(arr5, 0, 9);
    printf("Test Case 5 - Sorted array:   ");
    printArray(arr5, 10);

    // Test Case 6: Single element array
    int arr6[] = {42};
    int n6 = sizeof(arr6) / sizeof(arr6[0]);
    printf("\nTest Case 6 - Single element: ");
    printArray(arr6, n6);
    mergesort(arr6, 0, n6 - 1);
    printf("Test Case 6 - Sorted array:   ");
    printArray(arr6, n6);

    // Test Case 7: Two element array
    int arr7[] = {99, 1};
    int n7 = sizeof(arr7) / sizeof(arr7[0]);
    printf("\nTest Case 7 - Two elements: ");
    printArray(arr7, n7);
    mergesort(arr7, 0, n7 - 1);
    printf("Test Case 7 - Sorted array:   ");
    printArray(arr7, n7);

    return 0;
}
```

## Key Features Explained

### 1. **Merge Function**
- **Purpose:** Combines two sorted subarrays into one sorted array
- **Process:**
  - Creates temporary arrays for left and right subarrays
  - Compares elements from both arrays
  - Places smaller element in the original array
  - Handles remaining elements from either subarray
- **Memory Management:** Properly frees allocated memory to prevent leaks

### 2. **Merge Sort Function**
- **Approach:** Divide-and-conquer algorithm
- **Process:**
  - Divides array into two halves
  - Recursively sorts each half
  - Merges the sorted halves
- **Base Case:** When `l >= r`, array is already sorted

### 3. **Time Complexity**
- **Best/Average/Worst Case:** O(n log n)
- **Space Complexity:** O(n) for temporary arrays
- **Stable Sort:** Maintains relative order of equal elements

### 4. **Advantages Over Quicksort**
- Guaranteed O(n log n) performance
- Stable sorting (preserves order of equal elements)
- Better for linked lists and external sorting
- Predictable performance

### 5. **Disadvantages**
- Requires O(n) extra space
- More complex implementation
- Slower than quicksort for in-place sorting

## Compilation and Execution

```bash
gcc -o mergesort mergesort.c
./mergesort
```

